### Patient Evaluation: **Luna** (Cat | Pet ID: `PZ-PET-000002`)
⚠️ **Age Clarification Needed**:
You stated that Luna is **2 months old**, but PetZoe verified database records show his registered age as **1 year old**.
• **If Luna is currently a 2-month-old (8-week) kitten**, the pediatric protocol applies (deworming every 2-3 weeks until 12 weeks, then monthly until 6 months).
• **If Luna is an adult (1 year old)**, adult maintenance deworming applies (every 1 to 3 months or as guided by fecal examination).

• **Recorded Deworming History**: No deworming treatment recorded (Note: Absence of records indicates no treatment has been logged in PetZoe; it does not confirm the pet has never received care outside the platform).
• **Recorded Weight**: 4.2 kg *(accurate weight measurement on a clinic scale is required before dosing)*.

### Recommended Protocol for 2-Month-Old (8-Week) Kittens:
• **Pediatric Schedule (CAPC & WSAVA)**: Kittens should begin preventive deworming at 2 to 3 weeks of age, repeated every 2 to 3 weeks until 12 weeks of age (at 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, and 12 weeks), followed by monthly treatments until 6 months of age.
• **Target Parasites**: Young animals frequently contract roundworms (*Toxocara*) and hookworms (*Ancylostoma*) through mother''s milk (transmammary route) or environmental exposure.
• **Safe Pediatric Formulations**: Feline-safe anthelmintics include pyrantel pamoate, fenbendazole, or veterinary prescription spot-on treatments approved for kittens 8 weeks and older meeting the minimum body weight threshold.

### Critical Veterinary Safety Warnings:
• **Strict Feline Safety Alert**: NEVER administer canine deworming or flea/tick products to cats. Canine products often contain permethrin or concentrations that cause fatal neurotoxicity in cats.
• **Weight-Based Dosing**: Anthelmintics must be dosed strictly according to the animal''s exact body weight. Never guess or use human medications.

### Recommended Next Steps for Luna:
1. Clarify Luna''s current age with your veterinarian.
2. Have Luna weighed on a calibrated veterinary pediatric scale.
3. Book an appointment through PetZoe for a routine clinical examination, fecal floatation test, and a personalized prescription.', 'ASSISTANT', 'PATIENT_AND_VECTOR_KNOWLEDGE', 'patientContext+vectorRetrieval', 38, '[{"index":1,"title":"CAPC and WSAVA Guidelines for Deworming and Pediatric Parasite Control in Dogs and Cats (Core Puppy Deworming Schedule)","domain":"Companion Animal Parasite Council (CAPC) & WSAVA","url":"https://capcvet.org/guidelines/general-guidelines/","publicationDate":"Version 2024.2","retrievedAt":"2026-10-09T14:20:17.862992700","snippet":"Puppies should begin preventive deworming at 2 weeks of age.\nRepeat treatment every 2 weeks until 8 weeks of age (at 2, 4, 6, and 8 weeks).\nAt 2 months (8 weeks) of age, deworming is standard, essential, and strongly recommended.\nContinue monthly preventive treatments until 6 months of age, followed by year-round adult broad-spectrum heartworm and intestinal parasite prevention.","trustStatus":"VERIFIED_KNOWLEDGE","providerType":"VECTOR_PG"},{"index":2,"title":"CAPC and WSAVA Guidelines for Deworming and Pediatric Parasite Control in Dogs and Cats (Core Kitten Deworming Schedule)","domain":"Companion Animal Parasite Council (CAPC) & WSAVA","url":"https://capcvet.org/guidelines/general-guidelines/","publicationDate":"Version 2024.2","retrievedAt":"2026-10-09T14:20:17.862992700","snippet":"Kittens should begin preventive anthelmintic treatment at 2 to 3 weeks of age.\nRepeat deworming every 2 to 3 weeks until 12 weeks of age (standard intervals: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, and 12 weeks).\nTherefore, at 2 months (8 weeks) of age, deworming is strongly recommended and is an essential core component of routine preventive veterinary pediatric care.\nAfter 12 weeks of age, kittens should receive monthly parasite preventive treatments until 6 months of age.\nAdult cats should maintain year-round broad-spectrum parasite protection or be dewormed at intervals of 1 to 3 months based on lifestyle (indoor vs. outdoor) and veterinary fecal examination results.","trustStatus":"VERIFIED_KNOWLEDGE","providerType":"VECTOR_PG"}]');

⚠️ **Species Clarification Needed**:
Before administering any deworming treatment, **is your pet a puppy or a kitten?**

Because canine and feline parasite medications, active ingredients, and minimum weight thresholds differ significantly, you must confirm the species before a specific medication or protocol can be selected.

### Core Young Animal Deworming Guidelines (CAPC & WSAVA Standards):
• **Why Deworming is Crucial at 2 Months (8 Weeks)**: Almost all young puppies and kittens acquire intestinal parasites (roundworms and hookworms) from their mother''s milk or transplacentally before birth. Left untreated, worms can cause anemia, pot-bellied appearance, malnutrition, and diarrhea.
• **Kitten Schedule**: Deworming starts at 2-3 weeks of age, repeated every 2-3 weeks until 12 weeks of age, followed by monthly treatments until 6 months.
• **Puppy Schedule**: Deworming starts at 2 weeks of age, repeated every 2 weeks until 8 weeks, then monthly until 6 months.

### Critical Veterinary Safety Boundaries:
• **Never Cross Species**: Never give dog medications to cats. Many dog dewormers and flea treatments contain permethrin, which is fatal to felines.
• **Strict Weight-Based Dosing**: Safe deworming requires an exact, verified body weight on a pediatric scale to prevent toxic overdosing or ineffective under-dosing.
• **Never Use Human Medications**: Human dewormers or over-the-counter painkillers are dangerous or lethal to pets.

### What You Should Do Next:
1. Confirm whether your pet is a puppy or kitten.
2. Have your pet weighed accurately on a pediatric scale.
3. Schedule a consultation with a certified veterinarian on PetZoe to perform a microscopic fecal floatation exam and receive the correct, weight-appropriate prescription dewormer.', 'ASSISTANT', 'VECTOR_KNOWLEDGE_BASE', 'vectorRetrieval', 39, '[{"index":1,"title":"CAPC and WSAVA Guidelines for Deworming and Pediatric Parasite Control in Dogs and Cats (Core Puppy Deworming Schedule)","domain":"Companion Animal Parasite Council (CAPC) & WSAVA","url":"https://capcvet.org/guidelines/general-guidelines/","publicationDate":"Version 2024.2","retrievedAt":"2026-10-09T14:24:40.786120500","snippet":"Puppies should begin preventive deworming at 2 weeks of age.\nRepeat treatment every 2 weeks until 8 weeks of age (at 2, 4, 6, and 8 weeks).\nAt 2 months (8 weeks) of age, deworming is standard, essential, and strongly recommended.\nContinue monthly preventive treatments until 6 months of age, followed by year-round adult broad-spectrum heartworm and intestinal parasite prevention.","trustStatus":"VERIFIED_KNOWLEDGE","providerType":"VECTOR_PG"},{"index":2,"title":"CAPC and WSAVA Guidelines for Deworming and Pediatric Parasite Control in Dogs and Cats (Core Kitten Deworming Schedule)","domain":"Companion Animal Parasite Council (CAPC) & WSAVA","url":"https://capcvet.org/guidelines/general-guidelines/","publicationDate":"Version 2024.2","retrievedAt":"2026-10-09T14:24:40.787120","snippet":"Kittens should begin preventive anthelmintic treatment at 2 to 3 weeks of age.\nRepeat deworming every 2 to 3 weeks until 12 weeks of age (standard intervals: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, and 12 weeks).\nTherefore, at 2 months (8 weeks) of age, deworming is strongly recommended and is an essential core component of routine preventive veterinary pediatric care.\nAfter 12 weeks of age, kittens should receive monthly parasite preventive treatments until 6 months of age.\nAdult cats should maintain year-round broad-spectrum parasite protection or be dewormed at intervals of 1 to 3 months based on lifestyle (indoor vs. outdoor) and veterinary fecal examination results.","trustStatus":"VERIFIED_KNOWLEDGE","providerType":"VECTOR_PG"}]');


